ieve  full coverage when we talk about tuition  free public colleges and universities  there's a lot of issues that we can  thank Bernie for mainstreaming four  years ago but what I wonder is whether  the fact that those ideas have been  mainstreamed in some way hurts Bernie by  making the ideas no longer unique to  Bernie in the Democratic field well you  know it's interesting I think that being  authentic and being the originator goes  a long way with the public clearly  Bernie isn't just jumping on the  bandwagon he's promoting ideas that he  has deeply believed in and deeply fought  for for these past 40 years so I think  we welcome all these other candidates  supporting these incredibly good policy  initiatives and I look forward to Bernie  coming out with even more and better  ones  did you see the Town Hall that Bernie  did on CNN I guess now it was a couple  of weeks ago no I didn't you didn't okay  so I'll frame the question I glad I  asked so I can frame the question  correctly do you feel as though so far  the Democratic National Committee is  interfering in the primary in any way  similar to what we saw in 2016 or do you  believe that they are staying out of it  to the extent of picking a favorite or  leaning on someone or against somebody  else what's your assessment of their  presence and role in the primaries  so far I believe the primary so far you  know is just barely getting underway and  I think the DNC has been even-handed you  know I certainly am aware that there's  there's kind of two wings and the  Democratic Party one continues to be the  Hillary wing and you know I I imagine  that there's a lot of people on the DNC  that are that are in that wing which i  think is now going to become the Biden  wing but you know thanks to an  incredible amount of work by a whole lot  of people since the last Democratic  convention there have been some very  significant changes in the DNC you know  particularly in terms of super-delegates  not being able to vote on the first  round of voting at the convention and  and I also think that since the since  since their finger on the scale or shall  I say they're they're huge foot on the  scale was exposed last time around I  think they're a lot more sensitive to to  making sure that doesn't happen this  time yeah I hope you're right it's sort  of sad that it took this shameful public  thing that took place in order for some  of these rules to be changed and and for  hopefully things to be moved around but  I guess it's it's it's better than not  having the changes what one way or the  other you mentioned Joe Biden let me  talk about Joe Biden a little bit as I  told my audience last week Bernie  Sanders right now  is leading basically every poll for the  2020 Democratic primary specifically  among candidates who have declared Joe  Biden has not declared as of when you  and I are talking right now but in just  about every one of those polls Joe Biden  is polling ahead of Bernie  now there's one interpretation of those  poll  which is that's basically name  recognition and if Biden declares as  soon as people realize he's not actually  that progressive his support will go  down the other side is if if Joe Biden's  doing this well now when he declares  he's gonna run away with it  what do you think I think clearly the  the energy amongst the Democratic  rank-and-file actually get out there and  volunteer and work for candidates and  that donate small dollar donations to  candidates is in favor of Bernie and the  progressive wing of the of the  Democratic Party you know there's no way  that Biden is ever gonna turn out the  kind of crowds that that Bernie has  turned out and and actually if you look  at the most recent poll which I think  was by Emerson College amongst Wisconsin  voters Bernie even comes out ahead of  Biden specifically in within Wisconsin  you're saying that's correct  okay yeah I haven't I haven't seen that  one what do you think is responsible for  the strong polling numbers of Joe Biden  I think it's name recognition you know I  I think that you know if you just look  at even even Republicans and Democrats  everybody is saying we don't want  Washington insiders and Biden is you  know kind of the ultimate Washington  insider you know I have no doubt that he  will get a large hunk of big money  donations but big money donations also  known as bribery is not  what's going to sway the the voting  public this time around what will they  be swayed by will it be a particular  issue will it be personality will it be  who can best go up against Donald Trump  which is a consideration for some voters  well I think getting Donald Trump at  office as Bernie himself has said is  essentially job one and I think the way  to do that is to energize a huge base of  supporters and you know kind of mizzling  middle-of-the-road kind of centrist  policies is is not gonna do that you  know the other interesting thing is that  there's a significant overlap between  Trump voters and Bernie voters really  and and yeah and and it's not surprising  if you just listen to Trump's election  rhetoric last time around he was for the  little guy he was for the working people  he was not going to cut your Social  Security well Ben Heywood is it fair to  say I don't interrupt is it fair to say  Trump claimed to be those things maybe  is more precise exactly he claimed to be  those things and voters that wanted  those things had a choice of either  Trump the insurgent or Hillary the  establishment status quo business as  usual politician and and so they they  voted for God anything but the status  quo this time around I think Trump  voters many of them have seen that the  rhetoric does not match the reality and  if they truly want somebody who's out  for the interests of of the little guy  it's burning okay let's talk about  something that in some left-wing circles  I guess you're not supposed to talk  about but I really want to talk about it  with you particularly because of how  long you've known Bernie which is the  contingent of 2016 Bernie supporters  that either voted for a third party  candidate for Donald Trump or stayed  home now just so you know because you  may you know we our conversation was  years ago I supported Bernie Sanders in  the primary and ultimately cast a vote  for Hillary Clinton in Massachusetts  where I live in 2016  question one are you concerned about  that contingent in 2020 question two  what do you think drives that decision  to support Bernie Sanders in the primary  and then to do something other than  voting for the Democratic nominee when  it gets to the general election well to  support Bernie Sanders in the primary I  certainly understand that one okay  or you're suggesting you're positing the  the situation whereby Bernie does not  win the primary and and then would  people who voted for him in the primary  vote for a different Democrat it's not  so much I'm having it I'm sort of  thinking back to the small contingent  that did that in 2016 and the sort of  distant what maybe disproportionate  coverage of them given their actual size  well I think well firend last time  around nobody thought there was a chance  in hell that Trump was going to get  elected yes so they they didn't see a  vote you know they didn't see sitting  though sitting the election out creating  the problem that we've got today this  time around it's going to be pretty  clear to everybody that if you sit it  out we've got the the same horror that  we have today and ain't nobody gonna be  sitting in this election out yeah I  think that's a fair point I mean there  were some people last election who said  if it's not someone truly progressive  it's it's Hillary or Trump yes they're  sort of different but by and large it's  not effectively different now that we  see what's happened with the Supreme  Court we're seeing what's happened with  climate change or ignoring actually the  threat of climate change we're seeing  what's happening with the swamp feeling  you're suggesting that nobody at this  point would think that it's sort of  irrelevant who wins if it's not a truly  progressive candidate like Bernie that  people would realize the importance of  participating and voting no matter who  the nominee is well and and Bernie has  said it in so many words  that's four that he support whoever the  Democratic nominee is that the most  important thing we can do for our  country is to get Trump out of office  what is next for the campaign in terms  of messaging and strategy we know lots  of Bernie's most important issues  Medicare for all is sort of at the  forefront there what what shifts can we  expect in terms of the focus on issues  if any in the coming months you know I'm  not really aware of much that's coming  down the pike I mean you this is like  the question that I always get about  what's the new flavor coming out you  know if I knew I couldn't tell you you  know but I but I think what is really  different this time around and what what  is really exciting to me this time  around is the the diversity in terms of  race and gender and ethnicity in  Bernie's team and it is so wonderful to  be working with my fellow co-chairs  yulian the mayor of Puerto Rico Roe  Khanna and Indian by ancestry a  congressperson from  California and our the queen of the  co-chairs Nina Turner senator Nina  Turner from Cleveland an  african-american woman and it's it's a  beautiful thing to see and you know and  and I'm just so excited about the way  that Bernie has embraced diversity well  we have been following the campaign  closely and of course we will continue  to do it we've been speaking with one of  Bernie Sanders 2020 campaign national  co-chairs Ben Cohen really appreciate  you talking to me once again been good  talking to you David

